== 2. Unworthiness

=== The second #emph[device] that Satan has to keep poor sinners from believing, from embracing a Savior, is,

BY suggesting to them their unworthiness. “Ah!” says Satan. “As you are worthy of the greatest misery, so you are unworthy of the least crumb of mercy. What, do you think,” says Satan, “that Christ will ever acknowledge, receive or embrace such an unworthy wretch as you are? No, no. If there were any worthiness in you, then indeed Christ might be willing to be welcomed by you. You are unworthy to welcome Christ into your house; how much more unworthy are you to welcome Christ into your heart!” etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is seriously to consider that God has nowhere in Scripture required any worthiness in man before believing in Christ. If you#super[+] make a diligent search through all of Scripture, you#super[+] will not find, from#footnote[John 5:39.] the first line in Genesis to the last line in Revelation, one word that declares#footnote[Matt 19:8.] that God requires any worthiness in man before the soul believes in Christ, before the soul leans and rests on Christ for happiness and blessedness. Why then should that be a bar and hindrance to your faith which God nowhere requires of you before you come to Christ, so that you may have life? Ah sinners! Remember that Satan raises your#super[+] unworthiness as an objection against you#super[+] only out of a design to keep Christ and your#super[+] souls apart forever. And therefore, in the face of all your#super[+] unworthiness, rest on Christ, come to Christ, believe in#footnote[John 6:40, 47.] Christ, and you#super[+] will be happy forever.

The second #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is wisely to consider that no one ever received Christ, embraced Christ, or obtained mercy and pardon from Christ, but unworthy souls. Tell me, what worthiness was there in Matthew, Zacchaeus, Mary Magdalene, Manasseh, Paul and Lydia before their coming to Christ, before their faith in Christ? Surely none. Ah sinners! You#super[+] should reason like this: “Christ has bestowed the choicest mercies, the greatest favors, the highest dignities, the sweetest privileges on unworthy sinners. And therefore, O our souls, do not faint, do not despair, but patiently and quietly wait for the salvation of the Lord. Who can tell but that free grace and mercy may yet shine forth on us, though we are unworthy, and give us a portion among those worthies who are now triumphing in heaven?”

The third #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is this: if the soul will keep away from Christ until it is worthy, it will never take hold of Christ, it will never embrace Christ, it will never be one with Christ; it must lie down in everlasting#footnote[Isa 50:11.] sorrow. God has laid up all worthiness in Christ, so that man may know where to find it and may go out after it. There is no way on earth to make unworthy souls worthy but by believing in Christ. Believing in Christ will make#footnote[John 1:12.] you, who were slaves, worthy sons; it will#footnote[Jas 2:23.] make you, who were enemies, worthy friends, etc. God will count no one worthy, nor call anyone worthy, nor deal with anyone as worthy, but believers, who are made#footnote[Rev 3:4.] worthy by the worthiness of Christ’s person, righteousness, satisfaction and intercession, etc.

The fourth and last #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is solemnly to consider that if you#super[+] make a diligent search into your#super[+] own hearts, you#super[+] will find that it is the pride and folly of your#super[+] own hearts that drives you#super[+] to bring a worthiness to Christ. Oh, you#super[+] would gladly bring something to Christ that might make you#super[+] acceptable to him; you#super[+] are unwilling to come empty-handed. The Lord cries out, “Come, #footnote[Isa 55:1, 2.] all you#super[+] who are thirsty, come to the waters; and you#super[+] without money, come, buy, and eat! Come, buy wine and milk without money and without cost!”

“Why spend your#super[+] money on #footnote[Verse 2.] that which is not bread, and your#super[+] labor on that which does not satisfy?”

Here the Lord calls on moneyless souls, on penniless souls, on unworthy souls, to come and partake of his precious favors freely. But sinners are proud and foolish, and because they have no money, no worthiness to bring, they will not come, though he sweetly invites them. Ah sinners! What is more just than that you#super[+] should perish forever, you#super[+] who prefer husks among swine to the milk and wine, the sweet and precious things of the gospel that are freely and sweetly offered to you#super[+], etc.? Well, sinners, remember this: it is not so much the sense of your unworthiness as your pride that keeps you away from a blessed embracing of the Lord Jesus.
